<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Celtiberia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Celtiberia was the region of the eastern Meseta of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> inhabited by the Celtiberians, around the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a> and the southern tributaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>, roughly the modern provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a>, Guadalajara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teruel">Teruel</a> and Cuenca. Its towns, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segeda">Segeda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/contrebia-belaisca">Contrebia Belaisca</a>, resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for much of the second century BCE before the region became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> Tarraconensis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/celtiberia.jpg" alt="Celtiberia" width="320" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Ogre · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ethnographic_Iberia_200_BCE.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
